      "nets": ["RST", "SDA", "PWM", "SCK", "+5V"],
      "observed": "Both the ATtiny45 (U2) and ATtiny13V (U3) are included and powered, with corresponding reset, data, PWM, and clock pins tied to the same nets. For example, U2.6 and U3.6 both connect to PWM, and both VCC pins connect to +5V.",
      "expected": "The specification describes an ATtiny-driven fan controller, singular. Unless these are explicitly mutually exclusive assembly options, only one MCU should drive the shared signals; the unused MCU should be omitted or isolated.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "PWM : J4.1(MISO,passive) R2.1(passive) R5.2(passive) U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "+5V : ... U2.8(VCC,power_in) U3.8(VCC,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "RST : ... U2.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional) U3.1(~{RESET}/PB5,bidirectional)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "an ATtiny-driven fan controller"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If both listed MCUs are assembled, their GPIOs are electrically paralleled. Differences in startup or output state can cause contention and unreliable fan control; even if identical firmware is used, the design has no isolation or coordination between them.",
      "confidence": 0.87
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist contains two powered ATtiny devices whose corresponding GPIOs are tied together. That is unsafe if both are assembled; the files do not identify either MCU as a mutually exclusive or unpopulated option."
}

